</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Pilot study EM (%) across retrieval methods (<math id="S4.T3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}200" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>200</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}200</annotation></semantics></math>). No
retrieval outperforms all retrieval methods for every model.</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Across all models, the no-retrieval baseline outperforms every retrieval method tested.
The <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta</annotation></semantics></math>EM (dense <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>−</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-</annotation></semantics></math> none) was negative at every scale:
<math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-1.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-1.5</annotation></semantics></math>pp (360M), <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-11.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>11.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-11.0</annotation></semantics></math>pp (1.5B), <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-7.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>7.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-7.5</annotation></semantics></math>pp (3B), <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-2.5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>2.5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-2.5</annotation></semantics></math>pp (7B).
These observations raise a central question: is the problem that retrieval itself is noisy, or that small models struggle to use even high-quality passages?
The oracle experiment is designed to address this distinction directly.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Oracle Retrieval Fails for Small Models</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S4.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ 4.2 Oracle Retrieval Fails for Small Models ‣ 4 Results ‣ Can Small Language Models Use What They Retrieve?An Empirical Study of Retrieval Utilization Across Model Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and Figure <a href="#S4.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 4.2 Oracle Retrieval Fails for Small Models ‣ 4 Results ‣ Can Small Language Models Use What They Retrieve?An Empirical Study of Retrieval Utilization Across Model Scale" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> present our primary results
on corpus-matched questions (<math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n{=}756" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>756</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n{=}756</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div>
